{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And the garments of service (serad).</b> Its sense is as its Targum: לבושי שמושא — garments of service. And they would cover the vessels of the Tabernacle with them: there was a garment of תכלת with which they covered the Ark, and a garment of ארגמן with which they covered the altar, and a second garment with which they covered the table. And it would have been fitting to say first: 'And you shall make a garment wholly of תכלת to cover the Ark,' and a garment of ארגמן to cover the table." "<b>And behold —</b> 'Garments of holiness' (בגדי קדש), which he called by the definite article — he had already mentioned them above: ״ואלה הבגדים״ and so on. But בגדי השרד — why does he now call them by the definite article when they were not mentioned anywhere?" "<b>And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote</b> concerning this: 'I do not know why He did not first command to make the garments of serad, as He did with the work of the Tabernacle and the garments of Aaron and his sons' — end quote. And who is this that comes after the king? But with deference to him, I say that by way of the plain sense (peshat) it appears to me that the garments of serad were already alluded to in the section of the garments at the beginning of the pericah ואתה תצוה. For it first said ״ועשית בגדי קדש לאהרן אחיך לכבוד ולתפארת,״ and afterward said ״ועשו את בגדי אהרן״ in the language of definiteness, that is, the ones mentioned; and ״ואלה הבגדים״ also in the language of definiteness; and then He returned and said ״ועשו בגדי קדש לאהרן אחיך ולבניו,״ and did not say ״את בגדי הקדש״ as He said ״את בגדי אהרן.״ From here it appears that here He warned concerning other garments of holiness — they are בגדי השרד — which they shall make together with those of Aaron and his sons, all of which are called בגדי קדש. Therefore He repeated a second time, ״ועשו בגדי קדש,״ and this is the beginning of a separate command, just as He mentioned בגדי אהרן at the beginning of a command ״ועשו בגדי קדש.״ And the reason He called בגדי השרד 'בגדי קדש' is because they serve the holy vessels and cover over them; and for this reason He now called them by the language of definiteness, because they had already been included within בגדי אהרן. And the Rabbis, of blessed memory, wrote: בגדי השרד were made in the manner of curtains (קלעים), for we translate סרדין, and therefore they are called בגדי שרד." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ״ואת בגדי השרד״ — were it not for them, no remnant (שריד) or fugitive would have remained of the enemies of Israel. And so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded in tractate Yoma, chapter בא לו: a story in the days of Alexander of Macedon, that Shimon haTzaddik the High Priest wrapped himself in the garments of the priesthood and went out to meet him. When the king saw him, he descended from his chariot and bowed down to him. His servants said to him: 'A king such as you bows down to this Jew?' He said to them: 'The image of his likeness prevails before me in time of my battle' — and it was the angel who would appear to the priest in his likeness. And further there: בגדי השרד — garments that they would comb (גורדין) and work (משרדים) from them, that is to say, from spun thread that is not woven, and they would pierce it with a needle. And the Targum Yerushalmi: מעשה אורג עובד גרדי — the work of a weaver, a maker of gardi." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> ״את בגדי השרד״ — it is possible that the ה׳ in the word השרד alludes to the final ה׳ of the Name. And it is fitting that Scripture allude to it in בגדי השרד, for they are the covering of the holy vessels, and the holy vessels are covered and concealed within them and through them are revealed; so too the final ה׳ of the Name is a covering for the hidden Name, and through it there is revelation, and the discerning will understand."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:10.703447767Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}